  "query_preview": "..\n(Wednesday night through Monday)\nIssued at 302 AM EDT Tue Sep 22 2026\n\nThose of us that have been begging for some Fall-like weather will \nfinally be rewarded from Thursday through the weekend! A mid-level \ntrough will slide over the East Coast, with strong surface high \npressure over the Northeast and low pressure over the Atlantic. This \nwill create a typical 'wedge' setup into North and Central GA, with \nnortheast winds bringing in a much cooler and dry airmass. High \ntemperatures will crash into the mid to upper 70s on Thursday, then \nslightly warm back to the upper 70s to low 80s through the weekend. \nOvernight lows will fall to the mid to upper 50s each day, with \nupper 40s in the North GA mountains. Rain chances will be near zero \nthrough this time frame, due to the combination of dry air and \nstabilizing strong high pressure to the north.\n\nModel guidance remains in better than normal agreement for 7 days \nout, with most members showing the mid-level trough remaining over \nthe Mid-Atlantic for early next week. Northerly flow on the backside \nof the low would likely support a relatively dry airmass, with \ntemperatures gradually warming back up a bit.",
